    "title": "Record display for Hall of Trees at Mead Museum - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Gracie Terrall ,\n1 hours ago\nYANKTON, S.D. (KELO) – The halls are decked at the Mead Museum in Yankton for the 8th annual Hall of Trees display.\nOver 85 trees decorated by local businesses and individuals in the Yankton area are scattered about the three floors of the museum.\nStory continues below\n“It’s the most we’ve ever had, so it’s exciting,” Julie Mooney, the museum’s marketing coordinator, said in an interview with KELOLAND News.\nThe trees will be on display until December 31. The museum is open seven days a week from 10-5 Monday-Thursday, as well as Saturday, and open until 8 p.m. on Fridays. They are also open from 1-5 on Sundays.\nYankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Yankton’s 8th annual Hall of Trees Santa will make an appearance at the museum on Sundays from 2-4 p.m.\nAdmission is free for members and kids, $10 for adults, and $8 for seniors.\nOne of Mooney’s favorite parts about the Hall of Trees is seeing the community come together, both those who take part in decorating but also the guests who come to look at the trees throughout the season.\n“Most of (the businesses) had a minimum of two, maybe even five or seven people in a business that all get excited about what the style, what the theme of their tree was going to be,” Mooney said. “I think a community event like this creates great camaraderie.”\nEvery year, the museum has a theme for the front foyer, which boasts an Italian marble staircase and high ceilings. This year’s theme is 1950s Hollywood Christmas and features photos of old Hollywood celebrities and a red carpet.\n“Guests are spending a lot of time with the details in the foyer, they’re looking at the red carpet that has the Hollywood stars and starlets there, talking with people they’ve come with about what their favorite tree is, they are enjoying the hot cocoa, kids that have been here are running through the space,” Mooney said.\nEvery guest receives a token when they come to the museum to vote on their favorite tree. There’s also a scavenger hunt for kids– and adults.\n“Adults are enjoying that too because it just gives them more of a reason to spend the time looking at the details of the tree, so a scavenger hunt is a hit for all ages,” Mooney said.\nLast year, the museum saw around 3,300 visitors come for the Hall of Trees and Mooney says they are on track for another successful year.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to SiouxlandProud | Sioux City, IA | News, Weather, and Sports.",
